01.a|ADFJ|Build a four-column dossier for the parent's assignment-in-condition defect: expected admission relation, actual observations, inferred cause and untested alternative. Include a misleading successful input1 beside rejected-boundary inputs0and5.|One matching output validates the guard or a suspected cause is an observation.|The faulty guard accepts all supplied values; the dossier distinguishes observation from inference and does not claim a tool result before execution.|Use a stale configuration with correct source to produce the same visible admission error.|Choose an input on which the candidate explanations disagree.|The assignment changes the local value before the comparison. A nominal accepted input cannot distinguish correct validation from accepting everything.
01.b|BCDFJ|Create a replay manifest containing source, executable, symbols, input, configuration, working-directory assumptions and a finite timeout. Deliberately pair new source with an old executable and show the mismatch before diagnosis.|A source revision alone identifies the executed experiment.|The mismatched manifest rejects; a reproducible trial includes exact command, exit category and captured output without invented success.|Make an environment variable alter a parsing mode while all file hashes remain unchanged.|Include non-file inputs that affect semantics.|Hashes identify bytes but do not capture every environmental dependency or authenticate provenance. Reproduction requires a sufficient configuration boundary.
01.c|ADFIJ|Before editing, distinguish removed validation from input mutation in the parent's defined defect by observing the parameter immediately before and after the guard. Record predictions for both hypotheses and the smallest discriminating case.|Changing several things until the symptom disappears identifies the cause.|One observation distinguishes the hypotheses; the repair addresses the first contract-breaking transition and keeps a rejected alternative.|Diagnose an unchanged parameter with a corrupted capacity instead.|Ask what each hypothesis predicts at the same program point.|A causal explanation must survive a neighboring input that defeats a symptom patch. Observing final acceptance alone leaves multiple causes possible.
02.a|ADFIJ|Compile the intentional assignment defect with the selected warning set under both GCC and Clang, then parenthesize the assignment without changing its behavior. Preserve warning and runtime evidence separately.|Silencing a diagnostic repairs the code or Wall means every warning.|The parenthesized version remains semantically wrong; accepted options and actual versions are recorded, and debug/release observations remain separate.|Use an optimizer-eliminated local and explain a missing debugger value without blaming corruption.|A diagnostic describes a syntactic or semantic suspicion, not a full proof.|Compilers differ in diagnostics and defaults. A clean build still needs the admission relation and boundary tests.
02.b|BCDGJ|Extend the three-record diagnostic ring with a separate immutable first-cause field and a logging-failure seam. Inject input failure followed by cleanup and logging failures; preserve the original cause while the ring overwrites old events.|The last error is the root cause or a full log may erase required failure information.|First cause stays E_INPUT; last-three retention and dropped-record count follow the declared policy; no failure-path allocation is needed.|Allow two nested operations and define whether first cause is per-operation or global.|Separate causal status from diagnostic history.|The first-cause lifetime must be reset at the correct operation boundary. Global state can incorrectly carry one operation's failure into another.
02.c|ACDFJ|Create debug/release and logging-on/off builds with a diagnostic-only counter expression. Verify external validation and cleanup still occur with NDEBUG, while disabled diagnostic expressions do not execute.|Assertions and logging macros may contain required side effects.|All four configurations preserve functional admission/cleanup; only enabled diagnostics evaluate diagnostic-only work.|Replace the macro with an inline function and explain eager argument evaluation.|The call site's argument evaluation precedes entry to an ordinary function.|ISO C function calls cannot suppress evaluation of already supplied arguments. Macro and function APIs need different side-effect contracts.
03.a|ACDFJ|Define a same-failure predicate for an original bounded parser error, distinguishing timeout, malformed grammar, unrelated crash and the selected wrong transition. Replay each classification twice with reset state.|Every nonzero exit is the same failure and unresolved trials may count as successful deletion.|Only the selected symptom under matching identity is FAIL; unstable or unrelated outcomes are UNRESOLVED and never establish preservation.|Introduce a schedule seed and make replay identity include it.|Specify the predicate before running reduction.|Reduction is relative to the predicate. A coarse crash predicate can minimize to a different defect and invalidate the claimed diagnosis.
03.b|BCDFHJ|Implement subset/complement reduction for at most16 ordered chunks with a finite trial budget and cached classifications. Preserve grammar delimiters through an explicit chunk policy; compare trial order with the visible fixture without hardcoding its answer.|Arbitrary token deletion preserves the language or caching across configurations is safe.|Returned candidates retain FAIL, order and grammar; budget exhaustion is explicit and cache keys include input plus replay identity.|Use a grammar-aware tree reducer and explain which reductions it can now express.|A cache entry is valid only for the same deterministic experiment.|A reducer is not an oracle. Grammar-aware reduction changes the candidate space and minimality relation, which must be stated.
03.c|ABCFJ|Independently enumerate every one-chunk deletion of candidate[1,7,8], then enumerate the eight-chunk finite universe to find the smaller trigger[2,3]. Retain all tri-state outcomes.|One-minimal means globally smallest or unresolved deletion proves the remaining chunk causal.|The candidate is one-minimal under the declared predicate but size3 exceeds the global size2; unresolved outcomes limit causal interpretation.|Change allowed reduction from one deletion to two deletions and recertify minimality.|Minimality is relative to an allowed neighborhood.|No single deletion preserving FAIL does not mean every token caused the program defect. Reduction localizes an input, while code-state analysis establishes the repair.
04.a|ADFIJ|Use source and instruction stepping on the initialized admission counter to stop before the fifth acquire at capacity4. Compare the state before guard, after increment and after a later release.|The final valid count4 proves no invalid intermediate state occurred.|The first violation is the fifth admission reaching5; a reject-before-commit repair avoids it. Save actual debugger transcript separately from the model.|Add a callback between increment and clamping to expose the transient invalid state.|Stop at the boundary before the mutation.|A clamp can hide a violation from final-state tests while a callback observes it. Instruction stepping clarifies which transition occurred, subject to the actual generated code.
04.b|ADFIJ|Write caller and callee scopes each named status with distinct initialized values; select frames explicitly and inspect arguments versus locals. Include an inner shadowing block and record when its lifetime ends.|A debugger name denotes the same object in every frame or outside its scope.|The dossier identifies each live object by frame/scope and refuses an expired local as evidence.|Inline the callee and compare logical debugger frames with physical call frames.|Object identity needs more than a spelling.|Debug information may represent inlined scopes without a physical frame. The selected source scope and optimized availability must be recorded.
04.c|ADFIJ|Capture matched backtraces for debug and optimized builds, including an intentionally unavailable local. Pair transcripts with executable/symbol hashes and describe exactly what state is missing.|An optimized-out value was zero or a backtrace reconstructs all prior calls.|Unavailable values remain unknown; observed frames are not a complete execution history; mismatched symbols reject.|Use a tail call and explain why a source-level call may lack a retained frame.|A stopped stack is a current representation, not a history log.|Optimization, unwind metadata and stack corruption can all affect reconstruction. Distinguishing those hypotheses requires additional evidence.
05.a|ADFIJ|Choose a pure conditional breakpoint for used==capacity and a watch on the initialized live used object. Compare with a condition that calls a state-changing helper; reject that condition from the diagnostic protocol.|A breakpoint condition is observationally harmless regardless of its expression.|The chosen condition has no side effects and only accesses live storage; the helper alternative changes the experiment.|Replace the watched scalar with one field of a moving allocation and re-establish the watch after relocation.|A watch is bound to storage with a lifetime.|A logical object can move to new storage. Continuing to watch an obsolete address does not track the new object and may inspect invalid storage.
05.b|ADFJ|Relate the first actual watchpoint stop to the admission invariant and the source/instruction trace. Include a logical-state violation that changes no watched byte until a later publication.|The first observed value change is necessarily the earliest invalid state.|The dossier distinguishes violation, store and observation boundaries; stopping granularity is recorded rather than inferred.|Use a buffered event log whose flush occurs after the invalid transition.|Observation time and event time are different fields.|Hardware/software watches have different granularity and perturbation. A delayed observer can corroborate an effect without locating its origin alone.
05.c|ADFIJ|Request the parent's impossible watch width/slot combination in a model and attempt only valid bounded watches in the actual supported debugger. Record insertion/resume failure and choose a narrow watch or conditional breakpoint fallback.|An accepted watch command proves the hardware can install it or software watches cover all threads.|Unsupported requests remain unavailable; fallback changes the coverage claim and retains width, thread and lifetime limits.|Move the writer to another thread and assess whether the selected observation still applies.|Some resource failures appear only when execution resumes.|The finite geometry fixture is not the actual target geometry. Alternative instrumentation needs its own evidence and cannot be silently labeled equivalent.
06.a|ADFIJ|Map a real stopped PC to a source range in matched Og and O2 images, then inspect raw instructions around a moved/eliminated statement. Distinguish the synthetic line-map certificate from real debug metadata.|Source order forces instruction order or missing materialization proves a compiler bug.|The actual image supports only its mapped ranges; eliminated values stay unavailable and semantic behavior is checked separately.|Enable LTO across a helper boundary and repeat the mapping.|Use the executable's own debug information.|Source semantics constrain observable behavior, not a one-to-one instruction sequence. A diagnosis must address the failing optimized image.
06.b|BCDFIJ|Adapt the retained ABI probe to detect one deliberately unpreserved callee-saved register while stack restoration remains correct. Keep the faulty callee inside an original local test binary and test the corrected pair.|Restoring SP proves the whole calling convention or C source stack diagrams define frame layout.|The exact selected ABI register and alignment obligations are checked independently; the violating callee is rejected even with correct return value.|Change ABI or target and rebuild the list of obligations from its authority.|Separate each boundary obligation in the probe.|ABI conventions are not ISO C semantics. The harness itself must preserve its own calling convention and avoid assuming unspecified registers retain values.
06.c|ADFIJ|Pair a bounded saved-state record or permitted core with its exact executable/symbols. Ask for a deliberately absent heap region and distinguish unavailable state from a null/zero value.|A core contains complete memory and preceding execution history.|The absent region remains unknown; hash mismatch rejects; a saved model record is never renamed a native core.|Use a core from a different build with identical source line numbers.|Line numbers are not binary identity.|A snapshot supports state at a capture boundary, subject to capture omissions. Earlier causality needs replay or trace evidence.
07.a|ACDFJ|Diagnose separate original bounds, initialization, post-free and post-successful-resize cases using integer generation histories before any native negative run. Identify the first invalid transition and the required caller precondition.|All memory faults are the same mechanism or same-address resize preserves aliases.|Failed nonzero resize preserves old ownership; successful resize ends it; extent and initialized-value obligations remain distinct.|Change a physical out-of-bounds defect into a legal-address wrong-field write.|A logical invariant can fail inside valid storage.|A memory detector may miss legal-address corruption. The analysis must name the abstraction property rather than only the addressability boundary.
07.b|ADFIJ|Run isolated original negative/repaired controls in separate ASan/UBSan and unsanitized Memcheck builds where supported. Keep exact flags and reports, and include an initialization case ASan alone does not establish.|Every detector checks every memory property or a silent run is a proof.|Actual diagnostic, silence, setup failure and unavailable results are separate; no UB output is prescribed.|Move the invalid value through copies before consuming it in a branch.|A report can occur later than the first invalid source state.|Detector models and instrumentation coverage differ. The repair argument must precede the invalid transition, even when the report appears at a later use.
07.c|BCDFJ|Repair the first lifetime/extent transition, then inject failed allocation, successful relocation and an adjacent exact-capacity case. Compare full ownership state, not just disappearance of the crash.|Changing allocation size or adding a null check proves a lifetime repair.|The corrected contract preserves failure ownership and rejects the new boundary; stale aliases are not evaluated after lifetime end.|Introduce a borrowed callback view surviving beyond the call.|Track every borrower, not only the owner variable.|Clearing one pointer does not revoke all copies. A safe protocol must close each access interval or prevent publication of escaping borrows.
08.a|BCDFGJ|Repair the off=n cursor defect using the parent's seven-byte script, then split every positive result across a different boundary and insert bounded EINTR/EAGAIN events. Verify the exact emitted byte sequence.|A correct final byte count rules out duplicated or skipped bytes.|Offsets are cumulative, no byte repeats or disappears, impossible adapter counts reject, zero/EOF have operation-specific results.|Transfer to scatter/gather buffers with zero-length segments.|Track the accepted prefix of the logical stream.|A cursor is both a count and a position in the source representation. Vectored I/O needs a checked mapping between those views.
08.b|ACDGJ|Save errno immediately after a failing I/O call before a logging seam overwrites it; compare default and explicitly handled SIGPIPE in separate bounded child processes. Use an injected cap8 descriptor ledger for exhaustion.|Stale errno classifies successful calls or EPIPE is always observed as a return.|Original ENOSPC survives logging EIO; signal disposition controls the observable path; per-process EMFILE is distinct from system-wide exhaustion.|Use a pthread API returning a direct error number and adapt the wrapper.|Failure reporting conventions belong to each API.|An errno wrapper cannot be applied blindly to direct-status functions. Resource tests remain confined to owned descriptors and finite child processes.
08.c|ACDFGJ|Model Linux close releasing generation1 despite an error, followed by reuse as generation2. Reject an automatic retry that would close the new resource; repair the wrapper's ownership state and preserve error reporting.|Retrying every interrupted or failed close is portable and harmless.|The Linux model retires the old descriptor once; generation2 remains untouched. Record the different POSIX edition contract as a portability obligation.|Design an adapter with explicit close-policy selection and test both modeled contracts.|Descriptor numbers are reusable names, not persistent identities.|The wrapper must follow the selected OS contract. A runtime integer equality cannot establish that a reused descriptor denotes the old resource.
09.a|ABDFJ|Build wait-for and happens-before graphs for forced two-thread schedules; include a lock-order cycle and an unordered non-atomic conflict. State edge provenance rather than deriving ordering from timestamps.|Temporal order in a log is synchronization or every cycle is a demonstrated current deadlock.|Only synchronization supplies the relevant ordering; the stopped schedule corroborates a specific wait cycle, while an abstract possible cycle is a separate claim.|Add a timeout edge that can break waiting and reconsider the deadlock classification.|Distinguish dependency edges from completed synchronization edges.|A wait graph assumes particular resource and release conditions. Timeouts and cancellation can change liveness without repairing a data race.
09.b|ADFGJ|Force check-false, producer-set/signal, consumer-wait in the retained predicate program using bounded schedule gates. Capture whole-thread state and compare with the repaired same-mutex recheck loop.|A longer sleep or volatile fixes a lost wakeup.|The faulty schedule loses the event; repaired waiting atomically releases/reacquires and rechecks the predicate. No raced C value is prescribed.|Add a spurious wakeup and a second consumer.|The predicate is durable state; a notification is not stored work.|A signal does not imply the condition remains true for the awakened thread. The loop and ownership protocol must handle competition and spurious returns.
09.c|BCDFGJ|Test the synchronization repair under producer-first, consumer-first and cancellation-near-wakeup schedules, plus supported detector runs. Keep object destruction after all waiters are quiescent.|A detector-clean stress run establishes fairness or all-schedules safety.|Each forced schedule has a causal invariant argument; detector results cover executed schedules only; teardown cannot destroy a live synchronization object.|Introduce a third waiter and bounded queue ownership.|Teardown is part of the concurrent protocol.|Safety, liveness and fairness are different claims. More iterations do not replace the ordering and lifetime argument.
10.a|ACDFIJ|Decode the parent's basic PSP frame and vector arithmetic only after validating readable bounds, alignment and scoped EXC_RETURN assumptions. Add a truncated frame and an extended-FP marker that the basic decoder must refuse.|Any stacked word array is a valid basic frame or a plausible PC identifies the faulting access.|The original frame base/vector results hold under the stated model; unsupported/truncated frames remain undecoded, not guessed.|Add alignment padding with an explicitly inspected target contract.|First determine the frame shape, then interpret fields.|Frame validity depends on architecture and active configuration. An imprecise fault may not identify the responsible instruction even with a valid frame.
10.b|ADFGIJ|Discriminate bad startup range, invalid fault frame and DMA early reuse using safe captured status and ownership events. Mark side-effecting MMIO registers as unsuitable for blind debugger polling.|Reading all registers is observationally harmless or halting the CPU stops DMA.|The first invalid ownership event is CPU reuse before stop acknowledgment; invalid fault-address flags prevent using stale addresses.|Move the model to a cached MCU and list the added visibility obligations.|Use only status whose validity and read effects are known.|MMIO and independently running devices have target contracts beyond ISO C. Host mutexes and debugger stops do not automatically establish device quiescence.
10.c|BCDFGIJ|Repair cancel_request-to-CPU ownership so only confirmed stop or completion returns the buffer. Replay duplicate acknowledgment, timeout and reset histories in the portable model; keep hardware execution pending unless performed.|A corrected host trace validates peripheral timing and physical cleanup.|Early writes reject; one terminal transition returns ownership; reset requires an explicit quiescence contract and target evidence remains separate.|Add a late completion from the pre-reset generation.|A reset event is not automatically proof that every producer stopped.|Generation rejection protects logical identity, but actual DMA cessation and memory visibility require the selected hardware protocol.
11.a|ACDFIJ|Choose detector/static/fuzz tools for an extent fault, uninitialized value, race and defined saturation bug. Require a negative control for each claimed capability and record actual support rather than assuming installed means applicable.|A tool brand name is a complete verification strategy.|Each tool-to-property mapping names instrumentation/model limits; the defined narrowing error needs semantic comparison even if UB tools are silent.|Move code into an uninstrumented dependency and reassess coverage.|The property must be within the selected tool's model.|A clean run outside the checked property is not positive evidence for it. Unsupported combinations and missing dependencies remain explicit.
11.b|BCDFJ|Exhaustively compare all65536 uint8 operand pairs for the original saturating-add contract against a wide mathematical oracle. Preserve the first mismatch and reduce under a stated ordering without changing the contract.|Modulo narrowing implements saturation or one minimal pair is globally unique.|Expected is min(a+b,255); all overflow sums differ in the faulty implementation, and sum256 is the first failing sum under the declared ordering.|Change the contract to checked addition with unchanged output on failure.|The independent reference should not repeat the same narrowing expression.|A different failure contract requires a new oracle. Exhaustive finite pairs validate this domain, not arbitrary-width arithmetic or all integration uses.
11.c|ADFIJ|Triangulate a static alarm, a dynamic report and the minimized source transition for one original defect. Include a path infeasible under an explicit precondition and a dynamic trial that never reaches the fault.|Every static alarm is an executed vulnerability or tool silence disproves an alarm.|The dossier separates feasibility, observed execution and model assumptions, and identifies the evidence needed to close each disagreement.|Weaken the precondition and show when the previously infeasible path becomes reachable.|Write the assumptions used by each tool beside its result.|Disagreement can reflect different scopes rather than a broken tool. The contract and path reachability determine which claim is supported.
12.a|ADFGJ|Compare reject-before-commit with clamp-after-commit using an observer callback inserted between mutation and clamp. Show a neighboring case where both return the same final count but only one preserves the invariant throughout.|Symptom disappearance or equal final states proves a causal repair.|The callback exposes the transient violation in the clamp patch; the repair prevents the first invalid transition.|Replace the callback with an asynchronous consumer and derive publication ordering.|Choose an observation that occurs before the cosmetic correction.|A repair may need atomic publication or staging when intermediate state is observable. Final snapshots alone are insufficient.
12.b|BCDFGIJ|Replay the minimized trigger, adjacent valid/invalid boundaries, every acquisition failure prefix and debug/release/instrumented builds. Retain exact expectations for semantics and flexible categories for diagnostics.|Matching one error string or passing debug mode validates the repair.|Functional results and cleanup hold across declared builds; diagnostic wording is not the oracle; unsupported tracks remain open.|Change optimization and allocator policy while keeping the API contract fixed.|Separate semantic acceptance from detector observation.|The matrix should test changed assumptions that affect the mechanism. Repeating identical nominal tests under new names adds little evidence.
12.c|DFIJ|After at least seven days, diagnose a withheld variant with the same outward symptom but a different cause. Freeze predictions, replay manifest, first-invalid-state evidence and repair before revealing assessor analysis.|Delayed repetition of a known fixture is independent transfer.|The assessor scores causal reasoning and changed-condition repair; self-authored or previously opened variants retain limited independence.|Transfer the diagnosis to another component with a different ownership boundary.|Do not use familiar diagnostic wording as the defect identity.|Independence is an assessment condition, not a property of a passing test. The authoring repository supplies variants but cannot claim a learner has completed them.
